---
name: ptt-pr-reply
description: "Release the answer to a review of this branch's local request: carry out the plan drafted with /ptt-pr-draft-reply in this conversation (commits, tests) and record the reply. Without such a draft, asks first. Argument: none or the request number N."
disable-model-invocation: true
---

# Reply to a review

The command, or an AtE naming these records, approves the records this skill
writes; other work, such as changing the branch, needs AtE (rules file) or a
draft the user saw (below). You write only your own new message files:
never edit a message or move a request.

The branch is the one the user names; otherwise the branch of the worktree
this holder's task works in (`worktrees/<topic>`; if several, list them and
ask). Run every git command from the workspace root with `git -C
<worktree>`; never ask the user to change directory. Request numbers may be
short (`4`, `pr-4`, `000004`). Without N, the request is this branch's one
open request whose latest review needs an answer; if there is none or
several, list them (`<records>/bin/pr list all --branch BRANCH`) and ask.
With N, the request
must be in `pr/open`: if `<records>/bin/pr list all` shows it merged or
closed, report its state and decision and stop. A merged, rejected, or
withdrawn request takes no draft or reply; a pending draft of it lapses.
A follow-up is a new request (`/ptt-pr-submit`).

Read `<records>/bin/pr show N`, the latest review and its decision. Follow
the rules file's review timing: before the current review has a decision,
give only the author's stance per comment and stop, without a change plan
or execution question. A merge, reject, or withdraw decision ends the
reply even if the request has not yet moved. Continue only after a
decision requesting changes, checking that any draft answers this review.

After a draft of this request in this conversation (`/ptt-pr-draft-reply`),
this command, or an AtE naming it, approves exactly the drafted plan with
the user's changes: its commits, their tests, and recording the reply.
Write the reply from the agreed plan and its actual results. If carrying
it out needs more than the plan, stop and ask.

Without a draft of it in this conversation (also after `/clear`), do
nothing yet; ask:

```
No draft for request NNNNNN in this conversation. Decide:
1. Draft first (recommended): /ptt-pr-draft-reply NNNNNN; nothing written
2. Do the whole chain: change the code per the comments, commit, record the reply
3. No decision, discuss further
```

If several requests need answers, give an overview line per request, then
report one request per message (rules file). Use a numbered decision list
for one decision. For several decisions on that request, label each
question (`A1`, `B2`) and end with the `ALL1 AtE` line. The next request's
report follows the user's answer; keep decisions about different requests
in separate messages.

## Carrying it out

Carry out the approved plan after the decision requesting changes (rules
file, review timing); a reply pins the new head and returns the request to
"waiting for review".

1. Code changes go on the same branch: a change to an existing commit of
   the branch is folded into that commit, and only a new logical change is
   a new commit (rules file, atomic commits); the branch is checked as for
   submitting (`ptt-pr-submit`, steps 1-2): committed and clean (`git -C <worktree>
   status --porcelain` empty, `git diff --check <main> HEAD` clean), history
   atomic, and `git merge-base --is-ancestor <main> HEAD` succeeds; `<main>`
   is the request's repository's main branch in `<records>/pr/repos`.
   Before rewriting history, tag the old head `recovery/<reason>/<branch
   with / as ->` (`-2`, `-3` if taken), for `ptt-pr-cleanup` to remove
   later; after a rewrite give the new `base` if it changed, the old-to-new
   SHA map, and a `git range-diff <base>..<old head> <base>..<new head>`
   summary.
2. Write `MMMM-reply.md` (one more than the highest `MMMM`), answering every
   comment by ID from the agreed plan and its actual results
   (`C1: fixed in <sha>`, `C2: disagree, because ...`). List
   `MMMM-*` again; if another file has your number and is already
   recorded, rename yours to the next free one.
3. Record only it: `<records>/bin/ptt record -m "pr NNNNNN: reply MMMM" <file>`.
4. Report only the resulting commits, the verification, and any departure
   from the agreed plan. Show the reply text if the user asks.

## Template

`MMMM-reply.md`:

```markdown
---
request: NNNNNN
message: MMMM
kind: reply
author: requester
session: <holder name>
date: <YYYY-MM-DD>
base: <full SHA; only if the base changed>
head: <full SHA of the branch tip now under review>
---

# Reply

C1: <answer>

## Resulting commits
`git log --oneline <base>..<head>`; identify folds, splits, and additions.
For a rewrite, include the recovery tag, old-to-new SHA map, and range-diff
summary from step 1.

## Verification
What was re-run for the new head, with results.

## Departures from the plan
What changed from the agreed plan and why, or "none".
```
